A||||47||fiero56||sticking valve?||||5
Z||000000||fiero56||10-13-1999||10:19 AM||||I've noticed a noise that seems to me, to be a sticking valve.  How can I best determine this, without taking anything off, if possible?  This is my daily driver and I plan to drop the engine next month to replace my clutch.  I feel then would be the best time to do any other work.  If I continue to drive my car with that valve sticking for another month, am I going to hurt anything? What should be replaced to correct it?  -Thanks-||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-13-1999||10:41 AM||||What's the noise sound like?  If it's a light ticking that's loudest at idle, it might be a worn or dirty lifter (common on 4-cyl).  If it's a light ticking that loudest under acceleration, it's probably a leaking exhaust manifold gasket (common on 6-cyl).<P>If you had a valve that was actually sticking partway open, you'd notice a big drop in performance.  If you had a valve that was sticking shut, you'd hear some gawdawful noises and tear up the valvetrain pretty quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||10-13-1999||12:13 PM||||It's really kinda' hard to explain.  It sounds like a heavier ticking from inside the car when driving near a building(ie. in the garage), is more prominant under lighter acceleration and lower rpm, (i.e. going from a stop light while letting clutch out, small amount of gas, rpms around 1200-1500.)  When I'm driving down the road I don't hear it, but that may just be do to road noise and wind if the window is down, can't hear it if wondows are closed.  If the passenger side window is down, I can hear it when going from a stop light.  Can't hear it through drivers side window, unless I'm in the garage. It comes from the right side of the engine(battery side), and I can hear it pretty well if standing over the engine, gets louder if I tug the throttle cable a little, 1200-1700rpms.  Just so everyone knows it's an '88 Formula with 5-speed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||10-14-1999||09:00 AM||||It could be coming from your alternator or ac compressor or something in that area......<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||10-14-1999||09:55 AM||||Sounds like an exhaust manifold leak. Chadman is right, if you had a stuck valve you would not be able to drive the car.When you replace your clutch you will be able to see more clearly if the manifod is cracked. You might have to actually take it off since one of the manifo;ds that I had to repair actually came apart at the flange and it was on cyl. #2. You mentioned you were going to replace the clutch. Check out the clutch at <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A>  and click on the services button.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||10-14-1999||02:44 PM||||ok,<BR>Was huting around for the source of that noise with my stethoscope, aka "really long screwdriver". Nothing bad from the alternator, water pump, or block. Checked other bare metal areas I could reach.  It sounds like it's coming from the firewall side of the engine somewhere on the right side (near battery). I don't think it's an exhaust leak because it just sounds like too solid of a noise.  Not really a tick, but closer to a knocking.  Not reall hard, changes speed with the engine, but I only hear it closer to idle 900-1700 rpms.  I really doubt its a rod or similar "big" problem, but I'm stumped. I did find what I beleive to be a stray hose, here is a picture of it.  I am really doubting this is the cause of the noise. Haynes says its a hose coming off the EGR vacume control solenoid.  What does it connect to, and what can be hurt from it being disconnected.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Tof00003.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||10-14-1999||04:37 PM||||Hi,<P>My 85SE v-6 made exactly the same noise and I was sure it was a cracked exhaust manifold.  When I pulled the engine to replace the clutch I removed the exhaust manifolds and found several loose bolts.  The manifolds were not cracked.<P>I also found a small hole in the exhaust pipe where it was welded to my new catalytic converter.  It was on top of the pipe where I could not see it with the engine in the car.  The hole had eroded to around 1/16" wide by 3/8" long.  I'm pretty sure that and the leaky manifold to head fit up was the source of the noise.<P>I've welded the outside of the exhaust manifolds to the flanges and am going to port them out this weekend.<P>Good luck,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan||10-14-1999||05:57 PM||||Hard to believe it is a sticking valve.A compression check could determine that real quick,||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||10-14-1999||10:08 PM||||I did a compression check just a month or two ago.  I've never seen an engine with numbers as close as they were.  I don't remember the compression, but I recall they were within spec, and all were very close.  Damit, now I feel like a moron, I should've thought to check compression.  It will tell me if it's a valve real quick.  I'll try and check tommorrow.  Gotta stop and pick up a guage, my neighbor is out of town. He's got everything I don't.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-14-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||GWMORGAN||10-15-1999||12:01 AM||||Fiero 56<BR>        That loose hose from the egr solenoid is connected to a vacuum line which runs along the firewall ending just about in line with the #2 cylinder. If you look real good you can't miss it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||10-15-1999||02:25 AM||||Could very well be a sticking valve,cars drive just fine with valves hanging up a little from fuel deposits,just sound like they're going to die,(gosh,suprised the Ogre left this one alone after this long)and will allow the noise to occur at certain rpm.<BR>Make sure the crankcase is full,or  better,change it if it hasn't been done recently,and add some Marvel Mystery Oil,an excellent top end lube,to the oil. Have seen this before a few times.<BR>Either that,or could be a lifter,but if it started all of a sudden,then I'd say it's more likely a sticking valve,although a little exhaust leak,near the engine,can sound awfully close,but will likely sound worse when cold/on start-up,if this is the case. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||10-15-1999||09:54 AM||||I was waiting to see what Ogre had to say also, but he never spoke(I suspect he may be covering up, for his little ogre friends living in my engine, having their merry litle parties within my valves).  I'll try and check compression today.  If it is a valve problem, will the oil just solve my problem, or will I have to "go in" for some action?  I'll also try and find the other end of that hose today, spent about 15 minutes looking back there, couldn't find it, now I have a location to look in. Thanks for the help guys, hope I get it fixed today.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||10-15-1999||11:50 AM||||IF it is a stuck valve,from deposits,then yes,usually just good,clean oil,and an additive will do it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||10-15-1999||03:59 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I did. I sent the termites to test you all.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>no one considered one of the other common causes? a loose rocker arm......<P>Often if a valve is sticking, it's not to long before it gets burnt. A sticky valve or rocket sound about the same.<P>Could even have some crap built up in one or 2 cyls making it ping. Worse thing is if it's a ring going south. You didn't over heat it did you? That can toast out a top ring.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||10-15-1999||09:56 PM||||I did have a thermostat go bad a while before. Maybe 6 months ago. Got real close to red, I shut her down, and pulled the stat, drove home without it in.  No problems till just the other day, maybe Monday.  <BR>I just thought to add this(it's always done this since I bought it): when I start the car cold(in the morning) it fires immediatly, no smoke, perfect. But, when I start it up warm(usually within 30 minutes or so of shutting it down) it will crank for a good second before it starts and then blue smoke comes out for just a second or so.  I have heard everything about this, from: rings going bad, to leaky fuel injectors, to valves. This may sway where we're leaning for that noise.<P>Any of you guys om AOL Instant messanger? If you are look me up. I'm "fiero56".<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-15-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||WisconsinGT||10-15-1999||10:40 PM||||56...Something I learned from V8 Archie, is that GM cars in general, have a little oil buildup after running sitting somewhere. (Sorry, forgot the location.) It is not a problem according to V8 Archie. FWIW. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||WisconsinGT||10-15-1999||10:41 PM||||56...Something I learned from V8 Archie, is that GM cars in general, have a little oil buildup after running sitting somewhere. (Sorry, forgot the location.) So a litte bule somke on startup is considered normal. FWIW ||||reg||1
Z||000017||mwbackus||10-16-1999||01:30 PM||||Blue smoke on warm startup is usually an indication of bad valve stem seals, although it is also possisble for worn valve guides to do this.  It is not fuel injectors, a rich condition will produce black smoke, not blue. <BR>     Bad rings should show on a compression check, and the blue smoke will occur almost constantly while driving. <P>     Valve seals are quite inexpensive, and can be replaced without removing the heads.  This requires an air compressor with spark plug hole adapter (to prevent valve from dropping into cyl), and a decent spring compressor. <P>Mike                  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||10-16-1999||04:33 PM||||Is it possible for worn valve guides to make/allow the "knocking" sound I hear?  It is getting a little louder and is becoming more constant than when it had just started.<BR>I figure I should add, this knocking doesn't get louder with engine speed changes, it just changes speed with the engine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-16-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000019||AL||10-17-1999||01:44 AM||||before you change oil, say a week before, put in some tranny fluid in your engine.tranny fluid is the best for cleaning the engine, so if you have a valve that is noisy or stuck put it in and it will clean that plus the whole engine. leave i in ther for a week and then change your oil.good luck||||reg||14
Z||000020||fiero56||10-17-1999||04:36 PM||||I got that EGR line fixed this weekend. From the looks of the break, I think it ripped after the dogbone got some wear allowing for more engine movement. I have no idea how long that thing was broken.  Whats its purpose anyway?  I didn't get a chance to work on my "noise" this weekend.  Will a compression test tell me if it is a loose rocker arm?||||reg||14
Z||000021||Bruce||10-17-1999||07:07 PM||||Has anyone else used transmission fluid as an engine cleaner and, if so, what type and weight. I have an '89 Mazda with 238,000 miles and the only problem I have is that it makes the knocking noise during warm-up. Other than that, it's been a great car!||||reg||5
Z||000022||theogre||10-17-1999||08:30 PM||||I've heard more engine cleaning schemes than I can count...... I admit tranny fluid is a good cleaner, but to drive with it in for a week..... no thanks. I'd maybe let it sit and idle for a while, like 15-30 minutes, but I don't think I'd drive it.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Cooter||10-17-1999||08:42 PM||||I will probably get nuked by the ogre for this one- but. . . While working on a mid 80's Mustang with a plugged oil pump pick up, we poured it full of diesel fuel and kerosene and ran the engine until the sludge in the pickup disolved. Then drained the engine and put in some 20w50. Problem solved! I have used ATF before and just drove the car a few miles. If the engine is really gummed up inside, this could do more harm than good by breaking all the crud loose and it ends up in the pan. <BR>As far as the mysterious noise- How about a worn out lobe on the cam? My old 350 ate an exhaust lobe and it had a strange noise- sounded like a wrist pin knocking.||||reg||14
Z||000024||fiero56||10-17-1999||11:17 PM||||I don't think its a lobe on the camshaft , the engine has right around 150k miles on it, and this sound just started immediatly, nothing building up to it.  I don't hear it when I start the engine cold is one question.  I have two ideas: #1,  it may be just because the engine is running a little faster than idle, and #2: oil, when started cold, is thicker, when it warms up, oil is thinner,  allowing for something to "knock".  Note: the "mystery sound" doesn't change loudness under any conditions, it just keeps pace with the engine. Which is my reason for questioning the valve train. These are just my ideas.  To remove those valve covers requires pulling the intake etc.  I want to know exactly what I'm getting at, and fix it(without lots of downtime due to diagnosis), since it's my daily driver. Maybe I can make a wav file of it or something and put it on my page for you guys to listen to also.||||reg||1
