Z||000031||theogre||10-18-1999||11:53 AM||||I've heard of the kero/deisle cleaning too.... I definatly wouldn't drive it with that in there.<P>half a bottle of ATF added to the oil probly won't hurt anything.... Even driving it.... I think ATF is like 15-20 weight or something like that.... A half quart wouldn't thin the regular oil too bad. Had you bothered to say how much you were using......<P>As I've said I've heard allot of "methods" of cleaning engines. I've seen people do some major damage becuase they thought all they needed do was dump in something.<P>I've seen the kerosene trick done.... also saw some fool try to drive with it in.... not pretty. As long as it idles, the kero/diesle will lube the bearings, but it's to thin for any load like driving....<P>In any big cleaning.... it's not the crap that ends up in the pan that would worry me much.... Stuff in the pan generally sinks to the bottom. You'd have to suck up quite a bit to be trouble.<P>It's the flakes in oil passages that could block flow to bearings or return drians from the valve covers that would make me worry.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-18-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||AL||10-18-1999||08:15 PM||||ok guys, tranny fluid is used on trany's because they have small openings, better yet think of the trany like a vacuam system, alot of passages so the air(fluid) can pass thru, now when the filter gets dirty, the possibility of dirt or any other thing cloging the system is at it's best time to get clogged.so the fluid (tranny)is made in a special way, so if dirt does get in, it eats it up and keeps the passages open. so when you put it in your engine it quickly starts going to the sludge and whatever is in there and keeps it in the fluid, so when you drain the system, not only will you see black oil, you will see globs of oil comming out. so if you want to do this put in the specified tranny fluid that your car uses, and no it won't thin out your oil. so go at it and clean those engines.good luck,and remember only use half a bottle of it. ||||reg||1
Z||000033||AL||10-18-1999||09:04 PM||||forgot to mention use automatic transmission fluid, dexron or mercon only.||||reg||6
Z||000034||lowCG||10-18-1999||09:32 PM||||I'd be afraid my old tired motor would blow up if it weren't for the residual crud around the rings,holding them in position,and<BR>I still think I'd rather use a product that was chemically engineered to do a certain task,not that I really like additives or anything.<BR>ATF works to clean the lawnmower engine though.||||reg||7
Z||000035||AL||10-19-1999||12:28 AM||||you are forgetting that atf is lke a cleaner but is also very lubricating oil, so while it cleans it also lubes the engine.so if you buy those chemically enginered cleanrs they will clean your engine but also eat everything in it's path.to each his own i guess.||||reg||1
Z||000036||lowCG||10-19-1999||01:59 AM||||Al,you're probably right,just that I've never done any testing to whether or not the  film resistance per whatever dilution of normal oil the ATF works with.<BR>When you think about what an automatic transmission does with it's oil,it's really pretty amazing.||||reg||1
Z||000037||Patrick||10-19-1999||02:11 AM||||<BR>This may seem like a silly question, but I'm serious.<P>What is the real benefit of having an engine clean on the inside, other than making it easier to work on when it's been pulled apart. I realize that dirty oil is bad, but what harm does it do if crud is stuck to the inner walls of the block, etc. Is it because this crud will eventually block oil passages or what? ||||reg||5
Z||000038||lowCG||10-19-1999||11:17 AM||||No harm,as long as the residue doesn't block any oil returns,breathers,or cause hang- up/sticking in any of the hydraulic lifters.Like the Ogre said,usually just sits in the pan,on the bottom.||||reg||1
Z||000039||AL||10-19-1999||08:36 PM||||well guys the most imortant thing is to keep our fiero's running strong and clean.good luck||||reg||1
Z||000040||Dave85||10-19-1999||08:54 PM||||Once again, is it safe to use with Synthetic?||||reg||1
Z||000041||Groot||10-20-1999||07:33 AM||||Hi,<P>Well ... i have the same ticking sound !<BR>I did check compression and yes ....<BR>all 150 psi and the one ticking comes<BR>from is approx 100 psi .. too low !!!<P>What to do ? Replace the engine-oil ?<BR>Can i check the lifter ? A burnt valve<BR>does not tick does it ?<P>Please help !<P>Egbert ( 84-4cilinder )||||reg||8
Z||000042||Dan||10-20-1999||10:19 AM|||| When doing a compression check,It is not the value as much as uniformity.All cylinders should be within 15% of each other.So the 100 psi is low. Try bringing it up to operating temp and try again and observe the reading.On the 100 cylinder,try squirting motor oil in the cylinder.If the reading comes up, thats an indication the rings are worn.Oil helps to make a seal.If it doesn't,the problem will usually be in the valves.A burnt valve will not seal completely causing loss of compression.To further check it,bring the piston up to top dead center in the compression stroke an connect an air fitting.If the problem is valves, you should hear hissing at the exhaust or carb.Yes, when a valve doesn't seat completely,there will be excess distance for the rocker arm to travel because<BR>the valve is not fully seated.||||reg||1
Z||000043||Groot||10-20-1999||10:39 AM||||Hi,<P>When the engine is warm the compression<BR>is better and ticking sound is less.<P>I really think it's not the valve but<BR>the lifter mechanism. Suppose it is<BR>stuck how do i relieve it ?<P>Egbert||||reg||1
Z||000044||Groot||10-20-1999||11:14 AM||||Forgot to mention : the car ran only<BR>60Kmiles .....||||reg||1
